04 — Test plan, release gates, and rollback
Fee calculation tests
5 PI → 0.01 PI fee; recipient gets 5 PI.
7 PI → 0.01 PI fee; recipient gets 7 PI.
10 PI → 0.02 PI fee; recipient gets 10 PI.
50 PI → 0.10 PI fee; recipient gets 50 PI.
100 PI → 0.20 PI fee; recipient gets 100 PI.
First three withdrawals below 5 PI in one Africa/Lagos day → no fee.
Fourth and later withdrawals below 5 PI in that day → 0.01 PI each.
On the new Africa/Lagos day, the sub-5 allowance resets correctly.
Concurrent requests from the same account cannot both claim the same free ordinal.
An idempotent retry returns the same assessment and never creates a second fee charge.
Payment and accounting tests
Non-zero fee: the initiator pays the exact fee in Pi U2A flow.
A user-reported callback without successful server-side /complete does not count as paid.
Fee payment with wrong UID, wrong amount, wrong network, wrong metadata/assessment ID, or reused payment ID is rejected.
Fee-free withdrawal skips fee payment but follows normal authorization/reconciliation.
A2U payout never starts before required fee confirmation.
Recipient receives the exact requested amount; the fee is not deducted from net_amount.
Project reward reserve is debited by the reward amount actually paid, not reward amount + user fee.
Matured principal reserve is debited by principal actually paid, not principal + user fee.
Platform fee ledger records the fee only after confirmation; any refund/reversal is separately idempotent.
Payout and fee payment can be reconciled after a server interruption without duplicate payout or duplicate fee collection.
If fee is paid but payout fails, request is recoverable and is not charged twice.
Staking and liquidity tests
New duration choices accepted: 30, 90, 180, 365, 730, 1095.
Unsupported duration 60 cannot be selected for a new offer after policy rollout.
Existing accepted 60-day agreements remain unchanged and mature against their saved terms.
A stake is blocked if its full immediately withdrawable reward cannot be reserved.
Principal cannot be withdrawn before unlock_at.
Both reward and principal withdrawals below 0.5 PI are rejected by the database/API.
Minimum project baseline of 100 PI is enforced; higher dynamic required liquidity is enforced when liabilities demand it.
Project setup, construction, and operating-stage owner releases apply their approved stage risk settings.
Owner withdrawal is rejected if evidence/approval is missing, requester=approver, or post-release coverage fails.
One project's liquidity cannot cover another project's liabilities.
Pending/unverified incoming payments do not count as verified liquidity.
Missing project_treasury rows are not created without explicit project/wallet configuration and reconciliation.
Security and environment tests
Every new table has RLS enabled and no direct anon/authenticated write access.
Sensitive RPCs have fixed search_path, strict authorization, and narrowly limited EXECUTE grants.
All financial queries constrain network='testnet'.
Testnet tests make no Mainnet reads/writes and do not touch Mainnet services or secrets.
Idempotency keys prevent duplicate accounting entries.
Supabase security/performance advisors are reviewed after migration.
Frontend only enables withdrawals after end-to-end tests pass.
Rollback plan
Keep the new policy row inactive until all API and UI work is deployed and verified in Testnet.
If a release fails before enabling: stop at the feature flag, do not enable staking/withdrawals. The additive policy/assessment tables can remain unused or be removed only through a reviewed rollback migration.
If fee-payment or payout reconciliation fails: disable new withdrawal requests immediately; do not delete pending requests or transaction IDs. Reconcile them using Pi payment state and deterministic payout memo.
If coverage calculations are inconsistent: disable new stakes and owner capital releases; preserve existing accepted stake terms and allow only safe, reconciled settlement of already-due liabilities.
Never roll back by deleting ledger records or rewriting transaction history.
No Mainnet rollback is required because Mainnet is not in scope.
